\lispPackageChapter{sigma/truth}

The \texttt{sigma/truth} package provides a few small tools for converting and
negating truth values.

\section{Functions}

\lispFunctionSubsection{[?]}

The \texttt{[?]} function is Knuth's truth function.  It converts its argument
to $1$ for true and $0$ for false.  In other words, \texttt{nil} maps to $0$ and
everything else maps to $1$.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{([?] \lispVar{x})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
([?] nil) ; Returns 0
([?] t)   ; Returns 1
([?] 12)  ; Returns 1
\end{lstlisting}

\lispFunctionSubsection{toggle}

The \texttt{toggle} function returns the boolean negation of its argument:
\texttt{nil} if the argument is true, and \texttt{t} if the argument is false.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(toggle \lispVar{x})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(toggle nil) ; Returns T
(toggle t)   ; Returns NIL
(toggle 12)  ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}

\section{Generics}

\lispGenericSubsection{?}

The \texttt{?} generic converts a generalized boolean into a strict boolean:
\texttt{nil} stays \texttt{nil}, and any non-\texttt{nil} value becomes
\texttt{t}.

\lispSyntax

\texttt{(? \lispVar{x})}

\lispExamples

\begin{lstlisting}
(? nil) ; Returns NIL
(? t)   ; Returns T
(? 12)  ; Returns T
(? '()) ; Returns NIL
\end{lstlisting}
